ANDROID: arm64: stackprotector: always export boot-randomized __stack_chk_guard

Cherry-picked from CloudFox-Kernel abf87b62bfb43833e5f646564ec2e52b15b08472
(original message retained below). Applied by setup-clang.py only when
the clang override is on: stock clangs fail the sysreg stack-guard
probe, so PER_TASK stays off and this changes nothing for them.

---
Building this ACK 5.10 tree with Clang 19 changes the stack-protector
configuration: the sysreg stack-guard cc-option probe in
arch/arm64/Kconfig (-mstack-protector-guard=sysreg
-mstack-protector-guard-reg=sp_el0 -mstack-protector-guard-offset=0)
succeeds, so CONFIG_CC_HAVE_STACKPROTECTOR_SYSREG and, through it,
CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK (def_bool y) are now selected.  The
toolchain previously used for this tree did not pass that probe, the
per-task option stayed off, and the kernel kept the classic global
canary.

With CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK=y the compiler reads the canary
from current->stack_canary via sp_el0 and never references the global
__stack_chk_guard, which was therefore no longer defined or exported
(arch/arm64/kernel/process.c guarded it behind
!CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK).  The symbol disappeared from vmlinux
and Module.symvers.  Modules built against the previous configuration
rely on the classic global-canary mechanism and reference
__stack_chk_guard, so the OEM module environment could no longer
resolve the symbol and those modules would not load.

Keep defining the global __stack_chk_guard regardless of
CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK: it stays boot-randomized, exported, and
initialized to the same value as the initial task canary, restoring the
classic global-canary ABI for loadable modules.  Per-task protection is
left untouched and CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR remains enabled, so stack
protection is not disabled or weakened; the only change is that the
module-facing global canary is again available, boot-randomized exactly
as before.
---

diff --git a/arch/arm64/include/asm/stackprotector.h b/arch/arm64/include/asm/stackprotector.h
--- a/arch/arm64/include/asm/stackprotector.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/include/asm/stackprotector.h
@@ -36,8 +36,17 @@ static __always_inline void boot_init_stack_canary(void)
 	canary &= CANARY_MASK;

 	current->stack_canary = canary;
-	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK))
-		__stack_chk_guard = current->stack_canary;
+	/*
+	 * The per-task/sysreg stack guard (CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK)
+	 * lets the compiler obtain the canary from current->stack_canary via
+	 * sp_el0 and never references __stack_chk_guard.  Keep the global
+	 * __stack_chk_guard exported and boot-randomized anyway: it is part of
+	 * the GKI ABI/KMI and is referenced by loadable modules that use the
+	 * classic global-canary mechanism.  Setting it once, early, to the same
+	 * value as the initial task canary preserves the non-per-task semantics
+	 * while keeping per-task protection untouched.
+	 */
+	__stack_chk_guard = canary;
 #endif
 	ptrauth_thread_init_kernel(current);
 	ptrauth_thread_switch_kernel(current);
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/process.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/process.c
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/process.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/process.c
@@ -59,7 +59,7 @@
 #include <asm/pointer_auth.h>
 #include <asm/stacktrace.h>

-#if defined(CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR) && !defined(CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK)
+#if defined(CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR)
 #include <linux/stackprotector.h>
 unsigned long __stack_chk_guard __ro_after_init;
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(__stack_chk_guard);
